      "problem": "Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe integer $47$ \n{{field-1}}\nthe additive inverse of $29$ modulo $74$ since $47+29$ {{field-2}} $(\\text{mod}\\,74).$",
      "worked_solution": "The additive inverse of $29$ modulo $74$ is the integer $0\\le x<74$ such that\n\n$$\nx+29≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,74).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that when we add our two numbers, we get a number that is *not* congruent to zero modulo $74:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}47+29 & ≡76 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,74) \\\\  & ≡74+2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,74) \\\\  & ≡2 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,74) \\\\  & ≢0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,74)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude the following:\n\nThe integer $47$ $\\text{is not}$ the additive inverse of $29$ modulo $74$ since $47+29≢0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,74).$",
